% ============================================================================
% volcano-type — three named types of volcano and what each actually is
% (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% Not every volcano looks the same: a cinder cone is the simplest kind, a
% shield volcano is built almost entirely of fluid lava flows, and a
% composite volcano (a stratovolcano) is one of the grandest mountain-
% building kinds. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? volcano_type(shield_volcano, $D)   % built_almost_entirely_of_fluid_lava_flows
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `volcano_type(type, description)` carrying the source citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a volcano-related term that is not one of these three. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the type it
% names:
%
%     ? volcano_type($T, is_the_simplest_type_of_volcano)   % cinder_cone
%
% The three volcano types, as a little truth table:
%
%     type                 description                                       quote (verbatim)
%     -------------------  ------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     cinder_cone           is_the_simplest_type_of_volcano                   "Cinder cones are the simplest type of volcano."
%     shield_volcano        built_almost_entirely_of_fluid_lava_flows         "Shield volcanoes are built almost entirely of fluid lava flows."
%     composite_volcano     also_called_a_stratovolcano                       "Some of the Earth's grandest mountains are composite volcanoes—sometimes called stratovolcanoes."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from USGS's "About Volcanoes" page, which
% introduces each of these three types in its own complete sentence before
% going on to further description. WebFetch-verified before writing
% (twice — the second pass specifically confirmed each sentence's exact
% wording and where it actually ends, since a first-pass fetch can
% misjudge where a source sentence stops). `trust authoritative` — USGS is
% a primary .gov source, the same tier the sibling `rock-type.adj`/
% `mineral-hardness.adj` (the other libraries in this directory) already
% use for their USGS citations. A volcano-related term that is NOT one of
% these three — "lava_dome", a real term the same page also names, but one
% the source ITSELF explicitly disclaims as not a type: "A volcano can
% host many lava domes over a long period of time, so these are
% technically not a 'volcano type' but rather an eruption phenomenon." —
% is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing a description. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table volcano_type {
    columns type, description

    row (cinder_cone, is_the_simplest_type_of_volcano)
    row (shield_volcano, built_almost_entirely_of_fluid_lava_flows)
    row (composite_volcano, also_called_a_stratovolcano)

    source "Shield volcanoes are built almost entirely of fluid lava flows."
    locator "https://www.usgs.gov/programs/VHP/about-volcanoes"
    trust authoritative
}
